});
```
setSound(state.soundOn) with soundOn=true → gain 0.8 ✓ UI stays "sound on" ✓.

Also, the unlock path (nudge / any pointerdown) starts the audio while keeping the preference ✓ — that path is already correct.

But wait — the click handler fires on click, and pointerdown has already fired (the window's once listener) → startAudio() has already been executed inside unlock → by the time click runs, state.audioStarted is already true → it gets toggled to OFF! Bad. Sequence: user clicks the sound button: pointerdown → unlock → startAudio (context running, gain set to soundOn=true) → click handler: audioStarted is already true → setSound(!state.soundOn) = OFF.

Fix: track a "gesture consumed" flag: let gestureConsumed = false. In unlock: startAudio(); gestureConsumed = true. In the sound button click: if (!gestureConsumed) { startAudio(); gestureConsumed = true; setSound(state.soundOn); return; } setSound(!state.soundOn).

However, unlock fires on any pointerdown (including on the sound button) — so by the time of click, gestureConsumed is always true. Then the click would always toggle… hmm: on the first pointerdown anywhere, audio is unlocked with the current preference (on). So the first click on the sound button ends up toggling it off. That's actually acceptable UX (first tap anywhere starts the music; clicking the button turns it off), but a user who expects their "sound on" click to be an enable will find it confusing.

A better UX: the first gesture starts the music (anywhere). After that, the button toggles. That's standard (like the YouTube unmute). But if the user's first gesture is the sound button itself, they get muted. To handle this gracefully: if the first gesture is the sound button, don't toggle — just start (keeping the "on" state). Implementation:

```js
let firstGestureHandled = false;
const handleFirst = () => {
  if (firstGestureHandled) return;
  firstGestureHandled = true;
  startAudio();
  setSound(state.soundOn);
};
nudge/window pointerdown → handleFirst();
soundBtn click → { if (!firstGestureHandled) { handleFirst(); return; } setSound(!state.soundOn); }
```
But the sound button's pointerdown also fires window pointerdown → handleFirst runs before click → in click, firstGestureHandled is true → it gets toggled. Ugh.

Solution: in the sound button's click handler, check whether the audio was started by this button's own pointerdown… More simply: use a flag set synchronously in the button's own pointerdown (capture) before the window handler runs:

```js
soundBtn.addEventListener('pointerdown', () => { soundBtn._armed = true; }, true);
soundBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
  if (soundBtn._armed) { soundBtn._armed = false; return; } // pointerdown already started it
  if (!state.audioStarted) { startAudio(); setSound(state.soundOn); return; }
  setSound(!state.soundOn);
});
```
pointerdown (capture on the button) runs first → _armed=true; the window pointerdown listener also runs → unlock → startAudio() + setSound(state.soundOn) [gain on]. Then click → _armed → skip the toggle.

Any other click (nudge or the canvas): pointerdown → unlock → audio starts (on). Then the click handler: _armed is false, audioStarted is true → setSound(!on) → OFF! Also bad!

Hmm. The problem: a single click = pointerdown (starts audio) + click (toggles). Any click somewhere will mute the audio right after starting it.

The cleanest approach: the sound button only toggles when the audio was already started by a "different" gesture (i.e., started before this click's pointerdown). Track startStamp: audioStartedAt = performance.now() at the moment startAudio creates the context. In click: if (state.audioStarted && performance.now() - audioStartedAt > 300) → toggle; otherwise (just started by this click's pointerdown) → just update the UI.

This is robust and simple. Let me implement it:
- Add `let audioStartedAt = 0;` near the audio variable.
- Inside startAudio, after context creation: audioStartedAt = performance.now().
- Sound button click:
```js
